# Search autocomplete

Treat suggestions as optional. Build them only from records the person may see, and always let them submit their own query. For Home Assistant entities, use the native entity picker so names, permissions and keyboard behaviour stay consistent.

**Reference pattern.** The examples document a local adaptation. They do not add a publishing backend or a new Home Assistant capability.

## Keep suggestions optional

The [search-input component](../../components/search-input.html#search-field-filters-or-finder) owns labels and submission. The [search-filter pattern](../patterns/search-filter.html#share-the-filtering-contract) owns visible records and counts. This variant adds browser suggestions from the same fixed records. It does not implement the native entity picker or a predictive search service.

## When to use it

A small documentation catalogue usually needs a labelled search or filter, not predicted queries. Never invent entity IDs as suggestions. They must come from the permitted local dataset.

## Structure and behaviour

The examples use a labelled native input with a datalist filled from the same records shown below it. A submitted query filters the records by title and context. People can type a different query, open a record, see a no-match result and clear the filters. A polite status reports the result count without moving focus. Do not add custom combobox roles that conflict with the native control.

## Home Assistant boundary

These are local specimens, not a predictive query model. The browser decides how datalist suggestions look, so test keyboard and assistive-technology behaviour in the browsers you support. Filtering uses a small fixed dataset and does not query Home Assistant. No cloud model, query logging, storage or external search API is connected. Without JavaScript, all records stay readable and the submit buttons stay disabled.

